{
  "_provenance": "CAPTURED, not synthetic. Recorded 2026-08-18 from upstream OpenBMC firmware running under QEMU: image obmc-phosphor-image-bletchley-20260815025045.static.mtd from jenkins.openbmc.org job latest-master build 1714, booted on qemu-system-arm 10.2.1 as machine bletchley-bmc. The Redfish server is bmcweb, the production implementation, which has never seen this project. Nothing here was generated by this repository's mock. The board only appears once its FRU EEPROM is populated: a synthetic 144-byte IPMI FRU naming board product 'Bletchley -Class 1' and product 'Bletchley EVT' was written to the device tree's eeprom@56, then FruDevice and EntityManager were restarted. Without that step entity-manager never instantiates the baseboard and the walk is empty -- which looks exactly like a healthy capture of a sensor-less machine. What this proves: the walker survives real bmcweb, and the modern Sensors tree shape is recorded as production firmware actually serves it. What it does not prove: physical sensor behaviour, sensor-population density, real fault states, or any vendor Redfish dialect other than bmcweb. QEMU wires a subset of the board's devices, so the population here is partial by construction. The deprecated Thermal/Power shape is absent because current bmcweb does not serve it -- that half of criterion 2 is still proven only against synthetic fixtures.",
